sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch09-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Fossiliferous formations thick and extensive enough to survive later erosion are formed in shallow seas during periods of slow subsidence in which the supply of sediment roughly keeps pace with sinking.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Thick fossil-rich formations accumulate during subsidence";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter IX, “On the Imperfection of the Geological Record”; section “On the intermittence of geological formations”. Scope: marine sedimentary formations"
}
